reporter.raw:
{
  "single_story": {
    "yes": true,
    "note": "One story: Anthropic's Oct 7, 2026 launch of Claude Haiku 5.5. Same-day Sonnet 5.5 cache-read price cut and Max/Team API credits are part of the launch announcement and are covered as secondary facts."
  },
  "the_news": {
    "text": "On October 7, 2026, Anthropic released Claude Haiku 5.5, a small model it calls its cheapest, fastest and most capable yet. It is priced from $0.10/$0.50 per million input/output tokens (prompts up to 100K) and scores ahead of OpenAI's GPT-6 Luna on Anthropic's own benchmarks but well behind Sonnet 5.5 on most. [CLAIM: Anthropic says]",
    "ids": [
      "F1",
      "F2",
      "F3",
      "F4",
      "F5"
    ]
  },
  "why_it_matters": [
    {
      "text": "Anthropic cut small-model prices sharply: $0.10/$0.50 per million tokens for prompts up to 100K, down from $1/$5 for Haiku 4.5, and matching GPT-6 Luna's short-prompt rates per Kingy AI. Anthropic also cut Sonnet 5.5 cache reads by half the same day. The Decoder frames this as an escalating AI pricing war.",
      "ids": [
        "F2",
        "F3",
        "F8",
        "F11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The cheap model is not a drop-in replacement for bigger ones. Anthropic says Sonnet 5.5 and Opus 5.5 remain better for complex agentic coding, and independent commentary notes real savings may be smaller than list prices suggest because of a tokenizer change. The headline computer-use score is partial credit.",
      "ids": [
        "F6",
        "F7",
        "F10",
        "F12"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    {
      "id": "F1",
      "text": "Anthropic released Claude Haiku 5.5 on October 7, 2026, available on the Claude Platform (model string claude-haiku-5-5), Amazon Web Services, Google Cloud and Microsoft Azure.",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "Unite.AI",
        "The New Stack"
      ],
      "claim_by": null,
      "notes": [
        "The New Stack calls it the first new version of Anthropic's smallest model in nearly a year and Anthropic's third 5.5 model in a month."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "F2",
      "text": "Anthropic prices Haiku 5.5 at $0.10 input / $0.50 output per million tokens for prompts up to 100,000 tokens, and $0.50 / $2.50 for longer prompts; Haiku 4.5 was $1.00 / $5.00.",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "The Decoder",
        "The New Stack",
        "Unite.AI"
      ],
      "claim_by": null,
      "notes": [
        "Cache reads are $0.01 / $0.05 and cache writes $0.125 / $0.625 (short / long prompts)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "F3",
      "text": "Anthropic says Haiku 5.5 costs around 75% less to run on average than Haiku 4.5.",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "Unite.AI",
        "The Decoder",
        "The New Stack"
      ],
      "claim_by": "Anthropic",
      "notes": [
        "Per Unite.AI's reading of Anthropic's footnote: list prices are 90% lower for requests up to 100K tokens and 50% lower above, about 90% of Haiku 4.5 requests were in the lower tier, and the figure accounts for a new tokenizer that uses slightly more tokens per task."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "F4",
      "text": "Anthropic describes Haiku 5.5 as built for high-volume, cost-sensitive tasks such as summaries, compactions, database queries and classification, and as a subagent alongside Opus 5.5 and Sonnet 5.5 on coding work.",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "Unite.AI"
      ],
      "claim_by": "Anthropic",
      "notes": []
    },
    {
      "id": "F5",
      "text": "In Anthropic's own benchmark table, Haiku 5.5 beats GPT-6 Luna on every row where Luna has a score, including GDPval-AA v2.1 (1620 vs 1437), OSWorld 2.1 offline subset (72.4% vs 48.9%) and Terminal-Bench 4.0 (39.2% vs 16.4%).",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "The Decoder",
        "The New Stack"
      ],
      "claim_by": "Anthropic",
      "notes": [
        "These are Anthropic-reported results. The Kingy AI article says it has not run its own matched hands-on benchmarks."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "F6",
      "text": "Sonnet 5.5 stays ahead of Haiku 5.5 in Anthropic's table, for example Terminal-Bench 4.0 at 70.6% vs 39.2% and OSWorld 2.1 at 83.9% vs 72.4%; Anthropic says Sonnet 5.5 and Opus 5.5 remain better for complex agentic coding.",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "The Decoder",
        "The New Stack"
      ],
      "claim_by": "Anthropic",
      "notes": []
    },
    {
      "id": "F7",
      "text": "Haiku 5.5 is the first Haiku-class model with an adjustable effort setting (default medium).",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "The New Stack",
        "The Decoder"
      ],
      "claim_by": null,
      "notes": [
        "The default of medium is from The New Stack and Kingy AI."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "F8",
      "text": "Alongside the launch, Anthropic cut Sonnet 5.5 cache-read prices 50%, from $0.20 to $0.10 per million tokens, which it says makes Sonnet 5.5 around 20% cheaper on most agentic work.",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "The New Stack",
        "The Decoder",
        "Unite.AI"
      ],
      "claim_by": "Anthropic",
      "notes": [
        "The New Stack: some existing Azure and Google Cloud customers will have to wait a few days for the cut."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "F9",
      "text": "Anthropic is rolling out monthly API credits this week: $100 for Max 5x, $200 for Max 20x, and up to $500 pooled for Team subscribers, usable on any model. It also added beta computer-use and browser-use support to its Python and TypeScript SDKs.",
      "sources": [
        "Anthropic",
        "The New Stack",
        "The Decoder"
      ],
      "claim_by": null,
      "notes": []
    },
    {
      "id": "F10",
      "text": "Kingy AI notes the 72.4% OSWorld 2.1 score is a partial-credit score; the system card's strict pass rate (all checkpoints) is 37.1% for Haiku 5.5 vs 17.1% for GPT-6 Luna and 39.8% for GPT-6.1 Sol.",
      "sources": [
        "Kingy AI"
      ],
      "claim_by": null,
      "notes": [
        "Single source: figures are Kingy's reading of the Haiku 5.5 system card (Figure 8.9.3.A), which I did not open."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "F11",
